\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late, Won Resumes Downward Trend \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2000\par 11:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed late Thursday, with the South Korean won resuming its downward spiral after a two-day pause. \par \par \uc2  Persistently high oil prices, which hit a 10-year peak overnight, a slide in the Korean stock market and lingering concerns over the fallout of Ford Motor Co.'s aborted bid for Daewoo Motor conspired to push the won lower, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Concerns over high oil prices also contributed to the Thai and Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' descent to fresh lows since 1998, when the countries were mired in the regional financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  Bucking the region's weakness, the Singapore dollar and the New Taiwan dollar were slightly firmer. The Indonesian rupiah was little changed. \par \par \uc2  In the South Korean market, the dollar rose to 1,128.10 won from Wednesday's close of KRW1,124.80, ending the won's two-day rebound. The dollar jumped to an intraday high of KRW1,131.80 at the start of trading. \par \par \uc2  After tumbling to a 16-week closing low Monday on the Daewoo fiasco, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers are betting that the won could weaken further toward KRW1,140 to KRW,1,150 against the dollar in coming weeks. \par \par \uc2  The scuttled Daewoo deal has aroused concerns over whether this would hurt foreign capital inflows into South Korea, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  But analysts are preserving their longer-term bullish forecasts on the won, amid expectations of a gradual shift to a tighter monetary policy and the country's strong balance-of-payment position. \par \par \uc2  "I'm not looking for excessive weakness in the won," said Chi Lo, regional head of research and senior economist at Standard Chartered Bank in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  Chi said while he expects the dollar to hover around KRW1,130 at the end of the year, it will likely fall to KRW1,045 in 12 months' time. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered slightly after hitting a 27-month low of 42.77 baht to the dollar in early trading. Strong onshore dollar demand; spiking oil prices and mounting political anxieties ahead of the country's general elections, expected to take place in the fourth quarter, helped extend the baht's recent losses, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Despite dollar profit-taking and exporter selling of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , onshore demand for dollars remained solid as most corporates and state enterprises need to meet their debt repayment ahead of the Thai fiscal year-end and Japanese half-year book closing, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Reported remarks by a cabinet minister that the authorities may resort to short-term market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the baht if it is "unusually volatile" kept a reinon the baht's decline. \par \par \uc2  But few market participants took the nebulous warning too seriously, especially since recent remarks by senior officials suggested that the authorities are likely to tolerate the baht's current weakness. \par \par \uc2  Abhisit Vejjajiva, a minister in the Prime Minister's Office, was quoted as saying that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely adjust its policy if the exchange rate were to hurt inflation and economic growth. \par \par \uc2  "It's not a very clear-cut statement," said a chief dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  With no concrete signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , most market pundits remained confident that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could strengthen to the psychologically important level THB43 as early as next week. \par \par \uc2  Tracking the baht's descent, the Philippine peso ended at a new 32-month closing low of PHP45.995 against the dollar, compared with PHP45.910 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  For the first time since January 1998, the dollar muscled its way through the psychological resistance level of PHP46 to hit an intraday high of PHP46.04 in early trading. \par \par \uc2  Despite suspicions from some traders that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lightly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the morning, the authority insisted it wasn't in the market, and attributed the peso's losses to an "overreaction" to falls in other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the baht. \par \par \uc2  Given the raft of problems that have been undermining the peso - including a ballooning budget deficit and waning confidence in President Joseph Estrada's administration - many dealers expect the peso to depreciate further to PHP46.50 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In Taipei, last-minute heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  salvaged the New Taiwan dollar, which had earlier in the day languished on demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from importers and the regional curreny markets' weakness, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$31.263, down from Wednesday's close of NT$31.274. \par \par \uc2  Profit-taking in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped buoy the Indonesian rupiah and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 8,792 rupiah, little changed from IDR8,785 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at S$1.7460, almost unchanged from S$1.7462 late Wednesday, but up from Thursday's intraday low of S$1.7444. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9l04kit\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
