\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late On Long USD Liquidation; Bonds Up \b0\par\par\uc2 301 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2000\par 10:51\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was higher against its U.S. counterpart late Thursday, after banks and U.S. investors sold U.S. dollars on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains helped boost government bond prices. \par \par \uc2  Around 0842 GMT (4:42 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7445, down from S$1.7458 late Wednesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. dollar opened higher following its overnight gains in London and New York, and hit an intraday high of S$1.7480 in line with its early gains against the baht. \par \par \uc2  However, fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the psychologically important S$1.7500 level prompted banks and U.S. investors to sell their long U.S. dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said demand from local companies is expected to support the U.S. dollar around S$1.7435, with offers capping it at S$1.7485. \par \par \uc2  Government bonds had opened lower on selling in medium- to long-term maturities, in line with the Singapore dollar's early weakness, but the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's later gains encouraged market participants to start buying bonds, which ended higher. \par \par \uc2  Yields on the five-, seven- and 10-year bonds each closed one basis point lower at 3.79%, 4.20% and 4.54%, respectively. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the interbank money market, rates were steady on flush liquidity in\par the market.