\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Down Late; Baht Extends Fall \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 869 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2000\par 11:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The relentless fall in the Thai baht dragged most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower late Wednesday, as the respite in the region earlier in the day proved to be short-lived, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Investors continued to fret about the extent of damage persistently high oil prices would have on the region's economic growth and inflation. \par \par \uc2  "The market is still trying to come to terms with how much high oil prices will hurt Asian markets," said Vasan Shridharan, a treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank. "The three countries which will be most hurt are Thailand, the Philippines and Korea." \par \par \uc2  Indeed, worries about expensive oil prices continued to pressure the South Korean won. The won ended firmer, but it had relinquished most of its earlier gains, which were inspired by a Nasdaq-driven rebound on the Seoul bourse. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  countered the downward pressure from foreign fund outflows, helping keep the New Taiwan dollar steady. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, Southeast Asian central banks opted to stay on the sidelines as their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , led by the Thai baht, extended their southward trek, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar profit-taking by U.S. names earlier in the day had provided some reprieve for Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the grim political outlook in the Philippines, Indonesia and Thailand continued to haunt the market, dealers said. Concerns over the effect of rising oil prices on the Thai and Philippine economies also cast a pall on the baht and the peso, respectively, they added. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht's slide to its lowest level since late July, 1998, accelerated the fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its other Southeast Asian neighbors late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Comments by Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Assistant Governor Chetthavee Charoenpitaks late Wednesday that the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be trading in a range of THB40 to THB42 to the dollar failed to deter participants from pushing it below the key THB42.50 support level. \par \par \uc2  With no mention of actual, or planned \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to as high as THB42.63, and was trading THB42.590 around 0900 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Despite Charoenpitaks' remarks, the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to tolerate the baht's current trading range, unless it weakens too sharply, said Standard Chartered Bank's Shridharan. \par \par \uc2  But "they'll be mindful of any one-way trajectory of the baht" and a rapid depreciation, which could hurt Thailand's debt servicing, he added. \par \par \uc2  With the baht remaining depressed below THB42.50 at the end of the Asian trading day, the dollar could rise "very quickly" to THB42.75 to THB42.80 this week, a Thai bank dealer said, adding that a break above those levels could propel it toward the next upside target of THB43.20. \par \par \uc2  In Manila, the dollar closed at a record closing high of 45.910 pesos on the spot market, up from Tuesday's close of PHP45.875. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is poised to test the psychological resistance level of PHP46 if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't show any sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. The dollar had reached an intraday high of PHP45.950. \par \par \uc2  News that government forces rescued two French hostages held by Muslim rebels on Jolo island boosted the peso in early trading. But persisting domestic economic concerns which have undermined the peso for months - including worries over a ballooning budget deficit - subsequently overshadowed the good news. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped to 8,785 rupiah to the dollar, from IDR8,695 late Tuesday, as President Abdurrahman Wahid's decision to fire deputy commander of the Indonesian armed forces, Gen. Fachrul Razi, is expected to arouse further political controversy. The move came hot on the heels after Wahid sacked the national police chief Monday, following a bombing in the Jakarta Stock Exchange building last week which killed 15 people. \par \par \uc2  Unable to escape unscathed from the region's woes, the Singapore dollar weakened to S$1.7462 against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , from S$1.7455 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro's fall to a fresh record low also weighed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, but fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  tempered the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at $0.8462 after hitting a new all-time low of $0.8465 in Asian trade Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the South Korean won ended higher, extending Tuesday's rebound, as the local stock market leapt 6.1% following the surge on the Nasdaq, dealers said. Dollar-buying in the non-deliverable forward market and short-covering in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by local and foreign banks had, however, wiped out most of the won's earlier gains, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,124.80 won, down from KRW1,125.80 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at NT$31.274, barely changed from the previous close of NT$31.275. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounted for more than a quarter of the day's volume, which was valued at US$486.5 million, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9k04l1x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
