\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Down Late; Tracks Regional  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 ,Bourses \b0\par\par\uc2 448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2000\par 11:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was lower against its U.S. counterpart late Monday, as most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets fell on concerns over high oil prices and domestic uncertainties in some Asian countries, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0912 GMT (5:12 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7475, up from S$1.7418 late Friday in Asia. The U.S. dollar traded in a S$1.7450 to S$1.7488 range Monday. \par \par \uc2  Market participants ignored trade data which showed Singapore's non-oil domestic exports surged 27.3% on year in August, surpassing economists' forecast of a 15.3% rise, due to a steep rise in electronics exports. \par \par \uc2  With no sign of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the local dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose on buying by U.S. and European banks mainly on behalf of U.S. investment funds, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Fears of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at levels close to the psychologically important S$1.7500 level led to some profit-taking in the afternoon. But the U.S. dollar's brief retreat to S$1.7450 attracted buying by local companies, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Basically the market is short on U.S. dollars and if the authorities continue to stay out then we could see it go up much higher," the chief dealer at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Barring \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the de facto \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in London and New\par York trading, the U.S. dollar is expected to be capped around S$1.7500\par to S$1.7510 overnight, with support around S$1.7445 to S$1.7450, dealers\par said.\par - - 18/09/00 09-49G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Inflation worries stemming from high oil prices and a weaker Singapore\par dollar, combined with a fall in U.S. Treasurys Friday, pushed Singapore\par government bond prices lower while pushing up some interbank term\par interest rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Light selling of bonds, particularly the seven- and 10-year, was\par partly offset by some buying in the afternoon. The five- and 10-year\par bond yields ended up one basis point each at 3.83% and 4.57%,\par respectively. The seven-year yield ended flat at 4.22%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the interbank money market, the three-month rate edged higher while\par the overnight rate was easier on flush liquidity after the weekend.\par            Late Monday              Late Friday\par            bid      offer           bid     offer\par Overnight  1.7500%  1.8750%         3.0000%  2.2500%\par 3 Month    2.5000%  2.6250%         2.4375%  2.5625%\par 1 Year     2.7500%  2.8750%         2.7500%  2.8750%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9i04izi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
