\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Down Late; Thai Baht Hits 12-Mo Low \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2000\par 10:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  wilted late Wednesday on persisting regional political anxieties, the dollar's gains versus the Japanese yen and concerns over lofty oil prices, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht sank to its lowest level in almost 12 months earlier in the day, while participants tested the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's resolve as they pushed the Singapore dollar lower. \par \par \uc2  "There is a clear crisis of confidence in Southeast Asia, and it's hard to see anything (but) Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness, other than in the form of brief bouts of profit-taking," Bank of America said in a report. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pierced through its recent low of 41.63 baht to the dollar, which it touched at the end of July, and made a stab at THB41.70, its lowest level since end-September, 1999. \par \par \uc2  Dollar-selling by Thai banks and a large U.S. bank, however, stymied the baht's gains, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0800 GMT, the dollar was at 41.655, up from THB41.45 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar should be on its way to THB41.80 before it hits THB42, and then THB42.50 by the end of the year," a dealer at a Thai bank said. \par \par \uc2  Investors are expected to become increasingly nervous as Thailand's general elections, expected to be held in the fourth quarter, approaches, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers said. \par \par \uc2  After sinking below the psychological support level of 8,700 rupiah to the dollar, an explosion which rocked the Jakarta Stock Exchange accelerated the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide toward the end of the Asian trading day. \par \par \uc2  Around 0850 GMT, the dollar was at 8,775 rupiah, up from IDR8,560 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Fears the international community would isolate Indonesia if it fails to quell the violence in West Timor had also cast a shadow on the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, economics chief Rizal Ramli attributed the rupiah's losses to the diplomatic fallout from last week's killings of United Nations workers in West Timor and a weakening of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding that the weakness was temporary. \par \par \uc2  But the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't likely find any respite in the short term, with many participants in Singapore betting that the beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would spiral lower toward IDR8,800-IDR8,900, dealers said. Banks in Singapore were pushing the rupiah lower, they added. \par \par \uc2  And despite the looming threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the city-state's strong economic fundamentals, the Singapore dollar wasn't spared from the slump in the region. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar climbed to S$1.7445, from S$1.7390 late Tuesday. The U.S. dollar is expected to face stiff resistance around S$1.7450, dealers said. Even if it breaks that level, stop-loss selling expected around S$1.7480 will likely curb the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, a European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Despite the bearish undertone, some analysts warned against pushing the Singapore dollar and some of the other Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  much lower at current levels. \par \par \uc2  "We are wary of the market getting too ahead of itself," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg, adding that any good news could prompt the market to liquidate their long dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  In Manila, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  limited the peso's losses. The dollar ended Wednesday at 45.69 pesos, up from PHP45.60 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the New Taiwan dollar closed at an eight-month low, thanks to U.S. dollar-buying in the non-deliverable forward market and volatility on the local bourse, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$31.112, up from Monday's close of NT$31.095. Financial markets were closed Tuesday for a national holiday. Wednesday's finish was the lowest level since Dec. 30, 1999, when the New Taiwan dollar closed at NT$31.395. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9d04iry\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
