\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Down Late In Lackluster Trade; Bonds Steady \b0\par\par\uc2 360 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2000\par 11:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar tracked other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower against the U.S. dollar late Friday, while bonds ended steady, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0900 GMT (5:00 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7332, up from S$1.7306 late Thursday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S dollar rose to S$1.7330 in early trading on the news that Moody's Investors Service Inc. downgraded Japan's local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sovereign debt ratings to AA2 from AA1. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit briefly retreated to S$1.7305 on profit-taking amid fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , before rising again on speculative buying by interbank players. \par \par \uc2  "On the whole there is a biddish undertone to the (U.S.) dollar today, but the market has been quite dull," a European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to trade in a S$1.7300-S$1.7335 range in London and New York overnight. \par \par \uc2  On the bond market, government bonds remained steady amid light buying and selling of securities. Bond prices and yields were flat, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Most market participants preferred to square their positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The thin market activity "shows how small this market is," a local bank dealer said. "Many companies don't know ...they can get better returns than fixed deposits." \par \par \uc2  Yields on the five-, seven-, and 10-year bonds were at 3.84%, 4.20% and 4.56%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  The overnight interbank offered rate was slightly higher on pre-weekend fund demand and low supply as some lenders withheld funds for the weekend, a dealer said. However, the overnight rate is expected to ease to around 2%, higher than its previous closing levels this week, he added. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par            Late Friday              Late Thursday\par            bid      offer           bid     offer\par Overnight  2.6250%  2.7500%         2.0000%  2.2500%\par 3 Month    2.4375%  2.5625%         2.4375%  2.5625%\par 1 Year     2.6250%  2.7500%         2.6250%  2.7500%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 08/09/00 09-12G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9804fjc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
