\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Down Late On USD Short Covering; Bonds Lower \b0\par\par\uc2 396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2000\par 10:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was weaker late Tuesday following a region-wide short covering of U.S. dollar positions by banks and investment houses, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds also fell as a weaker local dollar triggered selling early in the session. \par \par \uc2  Around 0846 GMT (4:46 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7275, up from S$1.7220 late Monday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. dollar opened higher in Asia in line with its gains against the yen and the Thai baht. \par \par \uc2  It broke above the initial resistance at S$1.7250 on buying by a Hong Kong-based investment house, which propelled it to an intraday high of S$1.7280, a European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar subsequently retreated from the day's high on selling by local companies, he added. \par \par \uc2  "There generally has been good demand for U.S. dollars today," a second dealers said. However, fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  prevented market participants from bidding the U.S. dollar significantly higher, he added. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said London traders could bid up the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to S$1.7300 later Tuesday if it rises to Y106.70 from around Y106.32 currently. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to encounter initial resistance at S$1.7300 and then at S$1.7325, with support around S$1.7240, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, bond market participants sold longer-dated Singapore government bonds in early trading in response to the local dollar's weakness. However, market activity ebbed after the initial round of selling, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the benchmark 10-year bond closed two basis points higher at 4.56%, while yields on the five- and seven-year bonds closed one basis point higher at 4.21% and 3.84%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  On the interbank money market, interest rates were mostly steady on flush market liquidity. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par            Late Tuesday                Late Monday\par            bid      offer           bid     offer\par Overnight  1.1250%  1.7500%         1.1250%  1.5000%\par 3 Month    2.4375%  2.5625%         2.4375%  2.5625%\par 1 Year     2.6250%  2.7500%         2.6875%  2.8125%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 05/09/00 08-53G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9504b0v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
