\par\par \b \uc2  Bk Indonesia's Nasution Denies Singapore Helping Rupiah \b0\par\par\uc2 374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 August 2000\par 18:23\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- Acting Bank Indonesia Governor Anwar Nasution late Tuesday denied that Singapore authorities have sold dollars recently to help the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Market participants said they had seen evidence that the Government Investment Corp., the Singapore government's investment arm, has been selling dollars for rupiah in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  Last week, Nasution told reporters that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was cooperating with Singapore and other countries in the region to make sure banks didn't break rules governing maximum forward contracts allowed in rupiah. \par \par \uc2  But such cooperation didn't mean Singapore was selling dollars to help the rupiah, Nasution told reporters. \par \par \uc2  "The Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is a very liberal market," he said. "I don't think \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of us. Of course not," he added. \par \par \uc2  GIC's rupiah-buying may be linked to a possible investment in Indonesia in the near future, market participants have speculated. \par \par \uc2  Nasution reiterated that Bank Indonesia's increased supervision of forward trading didn't amount to capital controls. \par \par \uc2  "We're only checking. It's not capital controls," he said. \par \par \uc2  The acting governor also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will "take any measures that we need to take" to stop a slide in the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia can raise interest rates, and sell foreign reserves in the market, to stop speculative attacks on the rupiah, he said. \par \par \uc2  However, Nasution added that interest rate hikes alone won't help the rupiah if the political situation remains unstable. \par \par \uc2  A more stable political situation would also help encourage local exporters to bring dollar earnings back into rupiah, he added. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah has lost almost 30% in value since the start of the year, amid tensions within President Abdurrahman Wahid's coalition government. The dollar closed Tuesday in Asia at IDR8,965. \par \par \uc2  Investors remain loath to hold rupiah ahead of Wahid's state of the nation speech next Monday, which marks a major political test for his 10-month-old administration. \par \par \uc2  Although Wahid is unlikely to face impeachment, the session will form a rallying point for opposition, which blame the president for trying to consolidate his power over the coalition. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Wright; Dow Jones Newswires; 6221 3983 1277; tom.wright@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw8103rm7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
