\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Unlikely To Gain Much More On MAS Remarks \b0\par\par\uc2 727 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2000\par 10:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar jumped Thursday on news that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was prepared to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate, but it's unlikely to gain much more in the near term, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore "is prepared to allow a gradual and modest appreciation of the Sing dollar on a trade-weighted basis in the coming year, in line with the strengthening of economic fundamentals," Managing Director Koh Yong Guan said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar rose sharply on the remarks. The U.S. dollar fell to S$1.7341 from S$1.7413 shortly before the statement, which came at around 0355 GMT at a news conference to unveil the MAS annual report. Around 0820 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7377. \par \par \uc2  However, the Singapore dollar's rise may be over for the time being, analysts said. "I doubt we'll see markets get too carried away on the strong side for the Singapore dollar," said David Simmonds, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Simmonds and other analysts said they had expected the MAS to move toward a tighter policy stance some time this year to fight any sign of incipient inflation. Although the MAS has signaled its intent, it's not clear when the move toward a tighter policy will come. \par \par \uc2  Quoting Koh's remarks, Simmonds said that "'modest' and 'gradual' are important words." \par \par \uc2  In the next one to three months, Simmonds said, he expects the U.S. dollar to remain at the level of S$1.7300 to S$1.7400, rather than the S$1.7400 to S$1.7500 he forecast earlier. Simmonds's six-month target remains unchanged at S$1.6900-S$1.7000. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that despite Singapore's strong fundamentals - the economy is expected to expand between 5.5% and 7.5% this year - regional uncertainty will continue to weigh on the Singapore dollar. The woes of the Indonesian rupiah, in particular, are dragging down the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Our forecast is that the Singapore dollar will strengthen to around S$1.7300 by the end of the year," said Chia Hsin-li, market strategist at J.P. Morgan & Co. She called Thursday's gain in the Singapore dollar a "knee-jerk reaction" and not the start of a trend toward appreciation. \par \par \uc2  Thursday's statement comes days after the MAS was said to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar. Dealers said the MAS sold U.S. dollars for Singapore dollars at the level of S$1.7515 on Monday, sending the U.S. dollar sharply lower. \par \par \uc2  At Thursday's news conference, MAS Director Koh tacitly acknowledged the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "It's no secret that we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market from time to time when speculation and speculative elements bring the Singapore dollar out of line," he said. \par \par \uc2  The MAS uses the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than interest rates, as its primary monetary policy tool, seeking to maintain brisk growth and low inflation. The Singapore dollar is guided within a trading band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's major trading partners. \par \par \uc2  Koh said the MAS was prepared to allow a stronger Singapore dollar as a way of keeping inflation in check. Although inflation is expected to remain benign at between 1% to 2% this year, higher wages and commodity prices could produce inflationary pressures, he said. \par \par \uc2  Although the Singapore dollar has been vulnerable to movements in the more volatile rupiah, "We don't see any fundamental reason why that should be the case," said Khor Hoe Ee, MAS's senior executive director for economics. "Indonesia is doing well" despite the recent steep decline in the rupiah, he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say that the recent MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a sign that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar is already nearing the weaker limit of its trading band. As a result, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders were unwilling to bid up the U.S. dollar more. \par \par \uc2  "Basically the timing couldn't have been better," Philip Wee, treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank, said of Thursday's MAS statement. "Players were caught long on U.S. dollars," forcing them to sell, he said. \par \par \uc2  -By Amit Prakash and Christopher Wellisz, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4800; christopher.wellisz@dowjones.com; amit.prakash@dowjones.com. \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7r03nzw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
