\par\par \b \uc2  REPEAT:Singapore MAS Sees Incipient Inflationary Leaning \b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2000\par 06:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore is prepared to allow a modest appreciation of the Singapore dollar in relation to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its major trading partners, its managing director said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Although inflation remains benign for the time being, the MAS is concerned by signs that it may rise in the future, Managing Director Koh Yong Guan said. \par \par \uc2  "MAS is, therefore, prepared to allow a gradual and modest appreciation of the Sing dollar on a trade-weighted basis in the coming year, in line with the strengthening of the (country's) economic fundamentals," Koh said at a news conference to present the MAS annual report. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar rose within minutes on Koh remarks. \par \par \uc2  At 0415 GMT (12:15 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7375, down from S$1.7415 immediately before Koh's remarks were reported. \par \par \uc2  Consumer price inflation was zero in 1999, and rose modestly to an on-year level of 0.8% in June. For the year as a whole, the MAS expects inflation to come to between 1% and 2%. \par \par \uc2  But as Singapore's economy continues its brisk recovery from the Asian financial crisis, it will start reaching the limits of its capacity, and inflationary pressures will build, he said. \par \par \uc2  "We expect the remaining slack in the economy to be taken up within the next 12 months," he said. "As the labor market tightens further, wage pressures will begin to emerge." \par \par \uc2  In addition, rising world commodities prices will contribute to increases in the prices of imported goods, he said. \par \par \uc2  MAS said the gradual appreciation of the Singapore dollar would "contain any incipient inflationary tendencies without exerting undue pressure on our competitiveness." \par \par \uc2  Koh declined to specify a level to which the MAS may be prepared to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate. \par \par \uc2  The MAS uses the exchange rate, rather than interest rates, as its primary monetary policy tool. A stronger exchange rate reduces inflationary pressures by lowering the cost of imported goods in this trade-dependent economy. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar trades against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trade partners and is allowed to fluctuate within a band established by the MAS, which doesn't reveal the width of the band. \par \par \uc2  "As always, the exchange rate will be allowed to move flexibly within the policy band without undue or excessive fluctuations," the MAS said in its annual report, released Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Koh tacitly acknowledged that the MAS has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar in recent weeks. "It's no secret that we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market from time to time when speculation and speculative elements bring the Singapore dollar out of line," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar has weakened considerably against the U.S. dollar this year, in part, the MAS said, because of the demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Singaporean corporations making acquisitions abroad. But the Singapore dollar has gained against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Khor Hoe Ee, senior executive director for economics, said the current level of the Singapore dollar is "appropriate" on a trade-weighted basis. \par \par \uc2  He added: "The Singapore dollar at the moment is within the band we have set and we are comfortable with it." \par \par \uc2  -By Christopher Wellisz, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4802; christopher.wellisz@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7r03njz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
