\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Flat Early; Baht Up From 9-Mo Low \b0\par\par\uc2 415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2000\par 02:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are unchanged early Tuesday except for the baht, which is up fractionally from a fresh nine-month late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunged in late Asian trade, pushing the dollar up to THB40.805. Traders say foreign debt repayments by Thai corporates was the catalyst for the move, and stop-loss dollar buy orders accentuated its fall. \par \par \uc2  Because of the severity of the baht's decline Monday - it lost 1.2% - traders say the mild rally overnight from technical resistance isn't surprising. \par \par \uc2  In early Singapore trade, the dollar is at THB40.730. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar is unchanged early from late Monday at S$1.7441 after apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders say a Singapore bank that regularly acts as an agent for the authorities was a heavy buyer of Singapore dollars against its U.S. counterpart around S$1.7515 - a 22-month low - shortly before the end of the Asian session. \par \par \uc2  While the MAS never comments on market speculation, traders say it also supported the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week around S$1.7490. \par \par \uc2  "Perhaps it has drawn a line in the sand around S$1.7500 for now," said a U.S. bank trader in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah is unchanged early at 8,903 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Trade in the won, New Taiwan dollar and Philippine peso hasn't started. \par \par \uc2  In North Asia, foreign equity outflows, which led to sharp losses in local stock prices Monday, eroded the won and New Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, the dollar finished at KRW1,114, up from Friday's close of KRW1,112.70 as local stocks dived 5.8%. Won trade begins at 0030 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended at NT$31.046, up slightly from NT$31.019 as domestic stock prices fell 1.3%. \par \par \uc2  New Taiwan dollar trade begins in Taipei at 0100 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Domestic trade in the Philippine peso also commences at 0100 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso Monday was hurt by the baht's tumble and worries over domestic security, as riot police clubbed protesters ahead of President Joseph Estrada's state of the nation address. \par \par \uc2  A ballooning budget deficit also continues to hurt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended Monday at 44.600 pesos, up from PHP44.400 previously. \par \par \uc2  -By Benjamin Pedley, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4485; ben.pedley@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7p03lfi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
