\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Tracks Baht Down Early; Some Corp USD Buying \b0\par\par\uc2 302 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 2000\par 04:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar is lower early Monday, tracking a weaker Thai baht in light trading, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0227 GMT (10:27 p.m. EDT Sunday), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7484, up from S$1.7461 late Friday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that a weaker baht and some U.S. dollar buying by local companies pushed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a high of S$1.7488, but fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have prevented participants from testing higher levels. \par \par \uc2  "The previous high is S$1.7493, so players are afraid to push it higher than that," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the U.S. dollar to trade in a S$1.7460-S$1.7495 range Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. dollar is unlikely to break above S$1.7500 this week given the lack of strong upward momentum and fears that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  around there. \par \par \uc2  In a quarterly market outlook issued Monday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's DBS Bank said the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "unjustifiably weak" given Singapore's strong economic performance. \par \par \uc2  However, DBS Bank expects the Singapore dollar to remain haunted by regional factors, such as political uncertainties in Indonesia and the Philippines, and expects it to trade in a S$1.72-S$1.75 range in the third quarter. \par \par \uc2  But an expected weakness in the U.S. dollar in the fourth quarter, a more stable regional environment and a recognition of Singapore's economic strength will likely result in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciating to S$1.69-S$1.71 toward the end of the year, the bank said. \par \par \uc2  -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809; amit.prakash@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7o03ml6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
