\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Slightly Early On Weaker US Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 225 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 2000\par 04:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar is slightly higher early Friday after the U.S. dollar weakened against the yen and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  following a speech by Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan. \par \par \uc2  Around 0200 GMT (10 p.m. EDT Thursday), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7453, down from S$1.7460 late Thursday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S dollar weakened against the yen after a Thursday speech by Greenspan to a Senate committee that was interpreted as relatively dovish and suggests the Fed won't raise rates next month. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Singapore expected a quiet trading day Friday, barring further news of political turmoil in neighboring Indonesia. They said the U.S. dollar should trade well within its recent range of S$1.7420 to S$1.7480. \par \par \uc2  "All eyes are on the rupiah," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah weakened Thursday when President Abdurrahman Wahid refused to answer questions in parliament about his decision to fire two ministers. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar should be capped by concerns about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. But the U.S. dollar will be supported by brisk commercial demand toward the bottom of the range. \par \par \uc2  -By Christopher Wellisz, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4802; christopher.wellisz@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7l03jy2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
