\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Early; Dlr Below IDR9,100 \b0\par\par\uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 2000\par 03:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are mostly higher Wednesday, with the Indonesian rupiah extending Tuesday's rebound as investors continue to unwind their long dollar positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah's strength has supported the other Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which are firmer, dealers said. The New Taiwan dollar and Korea won are lower. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is at its lowest level in two weeks, falling below the 9,100 rupiah level. \par \par \uc2  Around 0115 GMT (9:15 p.m. EDT Tuesday), the dollar was at IDR9,095 compared with IDR9,365 late Tuesday and looks set to test the psychologically-important support level of IDR9,000. \par \par \uc2  However, dealers are doubtful the rupiah's gains will be sustained in the next few weeks as political uncertainties remain high ahead of the People's Consultative Assembly session in mid-August, when President Abdurrahman Wahid's performance will be reviewed. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Investors are also cautious ahead of an expected showdown between\par Wahid and the Indonesian parliament Thursday over his controversial\par sacking of two ministers earlier this year, dealers said.\par - - 19/07/00 01-31G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The rupiah's strength has provided some reprieve for the Singapore\par dollar, the Thai baht and the Philippine peso, all of which had been\par falling sharply over the past few weeks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Fears of another \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par after it apparently defended the Singapore dollar late Monday, is\par another factor that has buoyed the Singapore \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Around 0125 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7449, down from S$1.7462\par late Tuesday, and is expected to remain between S$1.7410 and S$1.7460\par Wednesday, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against the Thai \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the dollar was at 40.155 baht, down from\par THB40.255 late Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Philippine peso was at 44.455 pesos to the dollar, slightly\par stronger compared with Tuesday's close of PHP44.540.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Equity-related outflows have weighed on the New Taiwan dollar, which\par was at NT$30.983 to the U.S. unit, compared with Tuesday's close of\par NT30.960, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The South Korean government's issue of 800 billion won worth of\par five-year stabilization bonds, at an average yield of 8.13%, Tuesday\par continues to buoy the dollar against the won, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was at KRW1,113.50, unchanged from Tuesday's close, after\par rising briefly to KRW1,114 in early trade Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807;\par netty.ismail@dowjones.com\par -0- 19/07/00 01-45G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7j03i9e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
