\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Rupiah Recovery Aids Region \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 723 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2000\par 10:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed late Tuesday, with the beleaguered Indonesian rupiah regaining some ground as investors unwound their long-dollar positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah's recovery helped lift the Thai baht, the Philippine peso and the Singapore dollar off their intraday lows in late trading. \par \par \uc2  Around 0745 GMT (3:45 a.m. EDT), the dollar was at 9,365 rupiah, down from IDR9,520 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Those liquidating their long-dollar positions had probably given up waiting for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to break above IDR9,570 to IDR9,600, dealers said. The dollar had touched those levels last week, but rapidly retreated thereafter. \par \par \uc2  Fears that Indonesian President Abdurrahman Wahid would be impeached have waned, providing some reprieve for the rupiah, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers said. \par \par \uc2  "There's also a feeling maybe the Indonesian authorities are considering alternatives to limit the rupiah's losses," said Steve Brice, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered Bank. \par \par \uc2  State banks were said to have defended the rupiah Tuesday and local interest rates are expected to climb higher this week. \par \par \uc2  Political uncertainties, however, remain high and the rupiah's respite will likely be short-lived. Some analysts still predict that the rupiah will resume its downtrend toward the psychologically important IDR10,000 mark to the dollar ahead of the People's Consultative Assembly session in mid-August, when Wahid's performance will be reviewed. \par \par \uc2  "We'll still see a firmer dollar going forward," said Brice. \par \par \uc2  Strong corporate demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around IDR9,250 to IDR9,300 will likely provide strong support for the dollar, he added. \par \par \uc2  The mood is also cautious ahead of an expected showdown between Wahid and the Indonesian parliament Thursday over his controversial sacking of two ministers earlier this year, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht - which briefly touched a nine-month low of THB40.375 to the dollar on offshore dollar demand earlier in the day - recouped some of its losses, thanks to the rupiah's rebound and dollar-selling by local exporters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at THB40.255, up slightly from THB40.225 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso moved in tandem with the baht and the rupiah. The dollar was at PHP44.545, down from Monday's close of PHP44.590, and way below Tuesday's intraday high of PHP44.660. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had also pulled back against the Singapore dollar but remained well-bid despite the rupiah's recovery and suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Monday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Some investors appeared to be holding short positions in the U.S. dollar versus the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that they needed to cover, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was at S$1.7462, down from S$1.7489 late Monday, the level it was trading before the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in. \par \par \uc2  After having allowed the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drift lower in the past two weeks, the MAS, which rarely comments on its actions, was rumored to have defended the Singapore dollar around the S$1.7470 to S$1.7490 range to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Monday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Fears of another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely cap the U.S. dollar around S$1.7500 in the short term, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers said. \par \par \uc2  A 2.5% slide in the local stock market and the grim outlook for most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  depressed the New Taiwan dollar to its lowest level in almost eight months, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$30.960, up from Monday's close of NT$30.912. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won closed slightly lower following a government bond issue and amid fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by local monetary authorities, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The government warned last week it was prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the won from appreciating too sharply. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,113.5 won, up from Friday's close of KRW1,112.90. South Korean markets were closed Monday for a national holiday. \par \par \uc2  The ministry sold KRW800 billion in five-year won stabilization bonds, at an average yield of 8.13%, through an auction earlier Tuesday. The market had expected an average yield of 8.20%. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7i03his\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
