\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Steady Early; S$1.7450-S$1.7500 Range Seen \b0\par\par\uc2 303 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2000\par 05:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar is steady early Tuesday compared with late Monday's levels, amid light trading volumes, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0230 GMT, the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7473, still within the S$1.7470-S$1.7480 range seen late Monday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar rose in late Asian trading Monday, from a low of S$1.7495 to the U.S. dollar to the S$1.7470-S$1.7480 range, on the back of suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  was rumored to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Monday to cap the U.S. dollar under S$1.7500, but it remains well supported by buying interest around S$1.7450-S$1.7460. \par \par \uc2  "Many players are still short (on U.S. dollars), and we expect to see them buying on dips," a chief dealer at a European Bank said. \par \par \uc2  However, fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to cap the U.S. dollar around S$1.7500. \par \par \uc2  With the yen and most Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , except the Thai baht, remaining fairly quiet, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't expected to depreciate sharply, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the U.S. dollar to trade in a S$1.7450-S41.7500 range Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 421-4809; amit.prakash@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Corrected 0339GMT) 