\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Lower Late On Weak Regionals; Light Vol \b0\par\par\uc2 282 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juli 2000\par 11:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was sharply lower late Monday, amid U.S. dollar buying triggered by weakness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of neighboring countries, dealers said. Traded volumes were light, however. \par \par \uc2  Around 0920 GMT (5:20 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7495, up from S$1.7407 late Friday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Weakness in the Indonesian rupiah in early trading pushed the Singapore dollar lower, which was further weakened after the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell past the 40.20 baht to a U.S. dollar level in offshore trading. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's rise above S$1.7470 triggered stop-loss buying by banks that had earlier sold the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  short, a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  At the start of trading in London, banks there also bought U.S. dollar using Singapore dollars, pressuring the latter downward, the dealer added. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  has prevented the U.S. dollar's rise above the S$1.7500 psychological level. However, further weakness in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the yen could result in the S$1.7500 level being surpassed as early as Tuesday, they added. \par \par \uc2  "If the regionals and yen weaken further then it is unlikely the MAS will want to defend (the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," a second dealer said. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a U.S. brokerage said the next strong resistance level for the U.S. dollar is at S$1.7570. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the U.S. dollar remains well supported around S$1.7400. \par \par \uc2  -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 421-4809; amit.prakash@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7h03g59\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
