\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed; Won Retreats On Verbal  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2000\par 11:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed late Friday as investors braced for more volatility next week over the political uncertainty in Indonesia, following the recent frenzy in the market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was slightly firmer after plunging to a 21-month low against the dollar earlier this week, providing some respite for other southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the South Korean won ended slightly weaker on government comments that were supportive of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Profit-taking in long dollar positions smothered the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise against most southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Those who are long dollars are trimming their positions ahead of the weekend, while those who are short dollars are nervous," a dealer at a U.S. bank said. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won, which saw a rally this week despite the weakening yen and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , retreated on a verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the South Korean government. \par \par \uc2  The government's warning that it remains prepared to neutralize the impact of a potentially large foreign investment on the won weighed on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The comment was in reference to SK Telecom Co.'s (SKM) talks with NTT DoCoMo (J.NTX) regarding the potential sale of a stake in the company to the Japanese mobile-phone service provider. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asian dealings, the dollar was trading at 1,113.30 won, up from Thursday's close at KRW1,111.90. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the divergence between the resilient South Korean won and its underperforming southeast Asian counterparts is expected to persist. \par \par \uc2  Although the yen has had little impact on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently, investors are keeping a close watch on whether Bank of Japan will end its 17-month-long zero interest rate policy on Monday when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy board meets. \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, the dollar's failure to stay above the psychologically important level of 9,500 rupiah for the past two days prompted investors to unwind their long dollar positions, providing some relief to the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah had tumbled to 21-month lows Wednesday and Thursday, sinking to as low as IDR9,580 to the dollar Thursday before recovering above the IDR9,500 support level. At 0845 GMT Friday, the dollar was at IDR9,461, down marginally from IDR9,465 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "People are not willing to go long (dollars) around here and are selling off a bit before they buy up again," said a dealer at a U.K. bank. \par \par \uc2  The subdued trading in the rupiah tamed the movements in the Philippine, Thai and Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In the Philippines, the release earlier Friday of one of the remaining 20 hostages held by the Muslim rebel group Abu Sayyaf mildly supported the peso, dealers said. The dollar closed at PHP44.520 on the Philippine Dealing System, down from Thursday's PHP44.705. \par \par \uc2  In Thailand, corporate dollar demand caused the Thai baht to stay below the THB40 support level. Late in the Bangkok trading session, the dollar was at THB40.025, up from THB39.975 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at S$1.7425, after being stuck in a tight band of S$1.7407 and S$1.7431. Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unchanged at NT$30.877. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7e03gk5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
