\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dollar Up Late In Quiet Trading \b0\par\par\uc2 216 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2000\par 10:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar edged higher late Friday amid liquidation of long U.S. dollar positions in a generally quiet trading session, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0803 GMT (4:03 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7407, down from S$1.7421 late Thursday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded in a S$1.7408-S$1.7425 range through most of the Asian day. \par \par \uc2  "The market is consolidating ahead of the weekend and also the rupiah has been very quiet today," the chief trader at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said market participants sold U.S. dollars around the S$1.7425 level on fears that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around S$1.7440-S$1.7450. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, corporate demand for U.S. dollars around S$1.7390-S$1.7395 helped it to stay above the S$1.7400 level. \par \par \uc2  Barring a fresh round of weakness in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly the Indonesian rupiah, dealers expect the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to trade in a S$1.7390-S$1.7440 range Monday. \par \par \uc2  -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809; amit.prakash@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7e03ghx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
