\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Slightly Higher Early; Tight Range Expected \b0\par\par\uc2 203 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2000\par 04:24\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar is slightly higher early Friday, and is expected to trade in a tight range as regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets take a breather after a few days of hectic activity, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0202 GMT (10:02 p.m. EDT Thursday), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7418, down from S$1.7421 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Some participants who had gone long on U.S. dollars earlier this week sold them for Singapore dollars to lock in profits, a dealer at a U.S. brokerage said. \par \par \uc2  "The market is very quiet, but we expect U.S. dollar-buying to resume next week, particularly against the rupiah, peso and baht," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the U.S. dollar to trade in a S$1.7400-1.7440 range Friday. \par \par \uc2  Participants are expected to buy U.S. dollars at the lower end of the range and sell at the higher end because of fears that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the S$1.7450 level, a European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809; amit.prakash@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7e03g0b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
