\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Down Late; S$ Finally Succumbs \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 769 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2000\par 11:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly lower late Tuesday, with the Singapore dollar sliding to a 22-month low as it finally succumbed to the affliction borne by its other Southeast Asian counterparts. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso, however, continued to lead the downward spiral as it sank to a 30-month low. \par \par \uc2  Despite Singapore's relatively strong economy, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cracked below the psychologically-important S$1.7400 support level against its U.S. counterpart at the start of Asian trading. Market participants had cautiously bid up the U.S. dollar as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed no interest in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The last time the U.S. dollar was trading above S$1.7400 was on September 10, 1998. The U.S. dollar had attempted to break above S$1.7280 recently, but failed amid fears the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore might step in to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  With the sharp depreciation in the Indonesian rupiah, the Thai baht and the Philippine peso over the past week, the MAS is probably more tolerant of the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers said. \par \par \uc2  "The fall is in line with the weakness of the regional components of the Singapore-dollar trade weighted basket," said David Simmonds, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's rise above S$1.7400 triggered stop-loss orders. But lingering jitters \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at higher levels and the failure of the Thai baht and the Philippine peso to breach below key support levels tempered the Singapore dollar's fall, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0930 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7433, up from S$1.7385 late Monday, but down from Tuesday's intraday high of S$1.7436. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bank of America said in a report it expects the U.S. dollar to extend\par its rally to S$1.7450 and S$1.7500 thereafter.\par - - 11/07/00 09-55G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Philippine peso - which has set a weak tone in Southeast Asia all\par this week - skidded to a 30-month low of 45.150 pesos in afternoon\par trading as investors unloaded their pesos in favor of the U.S. dollar,\par given low local interest rates, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar closed at PHP44.820, off its intraday high as profit-taking\par in the U.S. unit set in, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A comment by President Joseph Estrada early Tuesday saying he hoped\par the peso wouldn't fall to PHP45 prompted many banks to lift their offers\par for the dollar to test that level, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bank of America said "a move to PHP46.30 now looks likely over the\par coming one to two weeks" as Estrada's comments provided the "green light\par for selling the peso for now."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Indeed, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had indicated it wouldn't aggressively\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up the beleaguered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We will let the market determine the rate," \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor\par Rafael Buenaventura told reporters shortly after the dollar breached\par PHP45. "We're not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar's rise above PHP45 stung the Thai \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which tumbled\par below the key psychological level of THB40 to the dollar, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Profit-taking by offshore participants and dollar-selling by local\par exporters, however, stymied the dollar's advance.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was at THB40.045, up from THB39.905 late Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Taking a breather after plunging to a 16-month low of IDR9,450 last\par week, the rupiah fell slightly amid heightened political uncertainty.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market participants held on to their long dollar positions amid doubts\par over the political future of President Abdurrahman Wahid, who will\par deliver his accountability speech to the People's Consultative Assembly,\par the country's highest legislative body, in mid-August.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was trading late in Asia at 9,338 rupiah, up from IDR9,293\par late Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The South Korean won distinguished itself from its Southeast Asian\par neighbors, ending higher as foreign investors sold dollars to invest in\par the local stock market, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar finished at 1,116.50 won, down from Monday's close of\par KRW1,118.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The bank strike, which started Tuesday, had little effect on the won\par given the low turnout and the fact that banks were able to continue\par their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The New Taiwan dollar ended unchanged, buoyed by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aimed at offsetting the impact of continued sluggishness in\par the local stock market, dealers said. The U.S. dollar closed at\par NT$30.842\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807;\par netty.ismail@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7b03dg9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
