\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Down Late But Off Lows; Tracks Baht, Peso \b0\par\par\uc2 286 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2000\par 10:44\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was at a 22-month low late Tuesday, but had partially recovered from its intraday losses after banks sold U.S. dollars to take profit amid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0805 GMT (4:05 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7415, up from S$1.7373 late Monday in Asia, but down from its intraday high of S$1.7436. The last time the U.S. dollar traded above S$1.7400 was Sept. 10, 1998. \par \par \uc2  After breaking resistance at S$1.7400 in early trading, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved higher in the afternoon on the strength of its gains against the Thai baht and the Philippine peso, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. dollar's failure to successfully breach resistance at 40.05 baht and 45.00 pesos triggered the selling of U.S. dollars for \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars to lock in profits, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the day's highs also prompted U.S. dollar-selling, they added. \par \par \uc2  "There was a rumor that (MAS) had come in, but the (U.S. dollar's) drop coincided with (declines against) the baht and peso," a European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "The (U.S.) dollar coming off was expected toward the end of the day, but it is likely to close above S$1.7400 (in Asian trading)," he added. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the U.S. dollar to trade in a S$1.7370-S$1.7430 in the near term. \par \par \uc2  