\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dollar Follows Baht Lower Late In Light Trade \b0\par\par\uc2 288 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2000\par 10:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar followed the Thai Baht slightly lower late Monday, amid light trading in a narrow range, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0757 GMT (3:57 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7373, up from S$1.7371 late Friday in Asia and higher than S$1.7364 early Monday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar weakened early due to some selling on weaker-than-expected U.S. non-farm payrolls data issued Friday and a stronger yen and Indonesian rupiah. But the dollar's rise above 39.80 baht briefly took the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the Singapore dollar to an intraday high of S$1.7385, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  However, corporate sell orders and fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at around S$1.7380 to S$1.7400 firmly capped the U.S. dollar below S$1.7380 for most of the Asian day, they added. \par \par \uc2  "There is some (U.S. dollar) buying interest around S$1.7353 to S$1.7355 and we have sell orders and MAS on the top," a European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The issue of data earlier Monday showing higher-than-expected second quarter on year growth of 7.7% in Singapore's economy failed to move the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but is expected to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from sliding sharply, the dealer added. \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. dollar could test the S$1.7400 level this week if last week's slide in Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809; amit.prakash@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7a03cf1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
