\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Dn:Uncertainty Prompts Dlr Demand \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 2000\par 12:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly weaker late Friday as uncertainty over events in Indonesia and other countries prompted investors to stock up on dollars ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  Economic and political concerns took their toll on the Indonesian rupiah, Thai baht and Philippine peso, which all finished the week at new lows for the year. \par \par \uc2  "There us a lot of uncertainty heading into the weekend and the market is responding by holding dollars," said Alison Seng, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard & Poor's MMS. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was unable to shake negative sentiment associated with a political battle between President Abdurrahman Wahid and his opponents in parliament. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the dollar was quoted at 9,302 rupiah, up slightly from IDR9,290 late Thursday. The rupiah has fallen nearly 35% since Wahid took office nine months ago, and some analysts say a further decline to IDR10,000 in the coming months is not out of the question. \par \par \uc2  The fall of the rupiah will "undermine Indonesia's outlook in terms of inflation performance, confidence and the ability of the country to service its external debt," Standard Chartered Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Steve Brice said in a research report. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was pressured by strong dollar demand, slow debt repayment by banks and the corporate sector, and political concerns, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the U.S. dollar was quoted at 39.705 baht, up from THB39.545 at the end of the previous session. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment was damped by worries that the next Thai general election expected this fall could leave a political vacuum that would lead to a slowdown in economic reform, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso fell below the key level of 44 pesos to the dollar for the first time in 21 months. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP44.030 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from Thursday's close of PHP43.950. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the peso tracked the weaker Indonesian rupiah and Thai baht. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Domestic concerns, such as lackluster economic growth prospects, the\par unresolved crisis in southern Mindanao and weak confidence in the\par Estrada administration, also continue to hurt the peso.\par - - 07/07/00 10-47G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In South Korea, a threatened strike next week by unionized bank\par employees put pressure on the won.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar closed at 1,118.80 won, up from KRW1,117.20 the day before.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Talks Friday between the government and union leaders ended\par inconclusively, although the two sides agreed to resume talks Sunday.\par Bank employees plan to strike Tuesday to protest the government's\par banking sector reform plans.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The failure of the two sides to reach a settlement spurred local banks\par to cover short-dollar positions, traders said. Separately, dollar-buying\par by overseas players in the non-deliverable forward market also pressured\par the won, traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Singapore dollar fell victim to the regional demand for U.S.\par dollars ahead of the weekend, dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Late in Asia the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7371, up from S$1.7360\par late Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said there was no sign of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary\par Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , even though the U.S. dollar briefly rose above\par last week's high of S$1.7380 in intraday trading.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, if the U.S. dollar rises much higher next week, perhaps above\par a key S$1.7400 level, the MAS may feel compelled to take action, dealers\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The New Taiwan dollar was unchanged against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Friday\par as U.S. dollar-selling by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  offset downward pressure on\par the local unit from ailing Taiwan stocks, dealers said. The U.S. dollar\par closed at NT$30.839.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires, 65 421-4859;\par alan.yonan@dowjones.com.\par -0- 07/07/00 11-07G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7703c5b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
