\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Down Late; Banks Shun Regional  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 383 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 2000\par 11:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was lower late Friday, reversing earlier gains, as banks sold Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for U.S. dollars ahead of the weekend, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0755 GMT (3:55 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7371, up from S$1.7360 late Thursday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to a low of S$1.7340 in early dealings on profit-taking, but rebounded to a high of S$1.7382 late afternoon as banks bought U.S. dollars ahead of the weekend, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The rupiah is bid into the weekend...(and) the general mood is still in favor of the (U.S.) dollar," a European bank dealer in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the de-facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though the U.S. dollar briefly rose above last week's high of S$1.7380 and is approaching the critical S$1.7400 level. \par \par \uc2  "MAS is unlikely to try and defend (the) S$1.7380 (level) now, but S$1.7400 is important and they won't want a close above it because it could spark bullishness," the European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  An Asian bank dealer said expectations of a modest acceleration in U.S. June non-farm payrolls, to be announced later Friday, also contributed to the U.S. dollar's strength. \par \par \uc2  In an afternoon research note, WestLB Bank analyst Syetarn Hansakul said strong U.S. employment data could further weaken regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as market participants will expect interest rate differentials to turn even more favorable toward the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  "For now I think there are offers capping (U.S.) dollar around S$1.7380 and it's supported around S$1.7340," the Asian bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Though a test of S$1.7400 is unlikely in London and New York trading later Friday, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market could have a volatile opening Monday if any unfavorable news emerge over the weekend, he said. He adding that a planned South Korean bank strike next week could also fuel market turbulence. \par \par \uc2  -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4807; amit.prakash@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7703bzp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
