\par\par \b \uc2  Most Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Lower; Rupiah Takes Biggest Dive \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 747 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 2000\par 12:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- A slew of negative political and economic news pushed most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  sharply lower Wednesday, with the Indonesian rupiah taking the biggest tumble. \par \par \uc2  An overnight bomb blast at the Indonesian attorney general's office, along with continuing political turmoil affecting President Abdurrahman Wahid's government, triggered rupiah selling that pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its lowest level in nearly 16 months. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, a second, unexploded bomb, was found at the attorney general's office and police evacuated the building while the device was removed. \par \par \uc2  The beleaguered rupiah started out the trading day on the defensive, after having hit a historic low the day before. Near the end of Asian trading Wednesday, the dollar was quoted at 9,305 rupiah, up from IDR8,600 Tuesday. It was the rupiah's weakest performance since March 12, 1999 when it slipped to IDR9,505 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  And with no resolution to Indonesia's political turmoil in sight, the rupiah is in store for continued selling pressure that could conceivably push it to IDR10,000 per dollar, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The rupiah was hit by panic-selling," a trader at a European bank said. "People were scrambling to get their hands on dollars," he said. \par \par \uc2  There were no injuries in Tuesday night's bombing in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  No one claimed responsibility for the bomb blast, but Indonesia's police chief didn't rule out the possibility that one of former President Suharto's sons was involved. \par \par \uc2  Senior government ministers have accused Suharto's supporters of inciting riots on the Maluku islands in an attempt to destabilize Wahid's nine-month-old government. \par \par \uc2  Economic Minister Kwik Kian Gie, meanwhile, said the rupiah's fall had nothing to do with the country's economic fundamentals. He predicted that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will strengthen to IDR7,000 per dollar after the annual session of the People's Consultative Assembly in early August. \par \par \uc2  In the Philippines, ongoing concerns over low local interest rates, political unrest in the southern island of Mindanao, and fears that the economy is slowing, kept sentiment firmly against the peso. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at a 21-month high of 43.690 pesos, up from PHP43.535 the previous day. The trading session was abbreviated because of heavy rains that caused flooding in some parts of Manila. \par \par \uc2  With the dollar climbing as high as PHP43.695 in intraday trading, dealers said they expected it to breach the PHP43.700 in the next trading session. \par \par \uc2  Falling regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also pulled the Singapore dollar lower. Late Wednesday, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7370, up from S$1.7327 late Tuesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  "The rupiah, baht and peso are all affecting the Singapore dollar even though its fundamentals are good," said Steven Brice, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Standard Chartered Bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  While the Singapore dollar approached lows it reached last week, dealers said it was unlikely the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with sentiment so strongly in favor of the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) would be like standing in front of a truck," an Asian bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won ended lower Wednesday amid worries about an upcoming strike at local banks and continued weakness in Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,117.20 won, up from Tuesday's close at KRW1,115.30. \par \par \uc2  "I don't see the strike as catastrophic for the won," Brice said. \par \par \uc2  "It is a negative because it could slow banking reform. And that is clearly something that is undermining sentiment domestically," he said. \par \par \uc2  Employees at local banks and bank-related financial institutions plan to go on strike on July 11 to protest expected mergers in the banking industry, which they fear will lead to large-scale layoffs. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht tumbled to a near nine-month low on the heels of the plummeting rupiah and concerns over a threatened walkout in parliament. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the dollar was quoted at 39.515 baht, up from THB39.335 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar was the lone Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to finish higher against the U.S. dollar, lifted by the rally in the stock market, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$30.814, down from NT$30.822 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires, 65 421-4859; alan.yonan@dowjones.com. \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw75039yx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
