\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Weaker: Wahid Comments Help IDR \b0\par\par\uc2 693 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2000\par 12:44\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly weaker late Tuesday, as market-soothing words from Indonesian President Abdurrahman Wahid helped stabilize the embattled rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Wahid called a news conference to refute reports that he was targeting opposition lawmakers as part of an investigation into corruption and riot-inciting in Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  Comments Wahid had issued over the weekend regarding the investigation were misinterpreted, he said. \par \par \uc2  "They (legislators) will be questioned only as witnesses. My other statement was twisted for political purposes," Wahid said at the news conference. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah, which had touched an almost 16-month low of 9,000 to the dollar in early trading, staged a modest recovery after Wahid's comments to finish the day at 8,600, unchanged from Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  Wahid nonetheless acknowledged that the political infighting and sectarian violence in the Maluku islands is taking its toll on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The rupiah weakness is because the market knows that the problem faced by Indonesia is very complicated, that there is disharmony among the people of Indonesia," he said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the rupiah will remain under pressure until Indonesia's political and economic situation improves. \par \par \uc2  They said market participants are concerned that continuing turmoil will prevent Indonesia from implementing reforms necessary to stay in the good graces of the International Monetary Fund and keep its economic expansion on track. \par \par \uc2  "We are worried that if this political mudslinging doesn't end or get better, confidence in Indonesia will be severely eroded and the IDR could be headed toward the 10,000/USD level in one month's time," said Syetarn Hansakul, economist at WestLB Bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso, which also is suffering from negative investor sentiment, also traded lower against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar climbed to a 21-month high of 43.600 pesos in intraday trading as the narrowing differential between U.S. and Philippine interest rates prompted investors to shift their holdings to dollars. \par \par \uc2  By the close, the dollar edged back to PHP43.535, up from PHP43.530 at Monday's finish. \par \par \uc2  Traders said concerns that the government may overshoot its budget deficit target and the unresolved crisis in southern Mindanao also weighed on the peso. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won declined on the heels of a weaker yen and fears of a strike next week by employees at local financial institutions, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Plus the won has been outperforming other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last few days so there is now some profit-taking," said Frank Gong, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,115.30 won, up from KRW1,113.80 a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  The won also took a hit from a drop in the Korea Composite Stock Price Index, or Kospi, which closed down 16.68 points, or 2%, to 818.53. \par \par \uc2  The softer yen and falling stock prices put pressure on the New Taiwan dollar as well. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$30.822, up from NT$30.812 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit dropped early in the session but reversed course after the Taiwan stock prices began to falter. Declines in the New Taiwan dollar were limited by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was seen selling small amounts of U.S. dollars throughout the day. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht closed slightly higher Tuesday as investors took profit on the back of the dollar's recent strong performance, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 39.355 baht, down from THB39.362 at the Asian close Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar finished lower as corporate dollar buying kept the U.S. dollar well bid, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Late Tuesday, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7327, up from S$1.7317 late Monday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Market participants were reluctant to push the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  much higher because of fears the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at levels above S$1.7340, a dealer at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 421-4859;\par alan.yonan@dowjones.com\par -0- 04/07/00 11-11G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw74038jd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
