\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 : Falling Rupiah, Peso, Lead Slide \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2000\par 12:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Investors expressed a general distaste for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday, with the Indonesian rupiah and Philippine peso bearing the brunt of the selloff. \par \par \uc2  Offshore selling of the rupiah was sparked by President Abdurrahman Wahid's comments over the weekend that he plans to crack down on legislators suspected of fomenting unrest in Indonesia, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  When markets opened Monday morning the rupiah immediately began declining against the U.S. dollar. By the end of the trading day the dollar had climbed to 8,940 rupiah, up from IDR 8,753 late Friday and the highest level in nearly 10 months. \par \par \uc2  Wahid's comments highlight the stress being placed on Indonesia's political system as Wahid attempts to restore economic growth and prevent separatist violence from tearing the country apart. \par \par \uc2  Cabinet Secretary Marsillam Simandjuntak, expanding on Wahid's comments, said Monday the president has suspended parliamentary immunity for at least 10 legislators being investigated. \par \par \uc2  He wouldn't say what the politicians were being investigated for, but there was speculation that Wahid's political rivals and critics were among those being investigated. \par \par \uc2  "What's happening to the rupiah has to do with the fact that Wahid is going after the legislators," said Vincent Low, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like there may be a standoff with parliament and that is causing concerns," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso, under pressure on a number of fronts, fell to a 21-month low of 43.580 per dollar during intraday trading before staging a modest recovery in late trading. The dollar closed at PHP43.560, up from 43.230 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders attributed the peso's decline to a host of factors, including a regional bias in favor of the dollar, low local interest rates, budget deficit concerns and rising oil prices. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Rafael Buenaventura went on television as the peso was falling and said he wasn't overly concerned about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline. \par \par \uc2  "It's still at a comfortable level at the moment," he said, adding that the peso's level should be "market determined." \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to head off speculative activity, which didn't appear to be occurring. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar gained some ground against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but remained below levels that would cause concern to local monetary authorities, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  was rumored to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  late last week when the U.S. dollar rose to S$1.7380, its highest level in more than a year. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of trading Monday, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7315, up from S$1.7271 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The current level shouldn't unduly alarm authorities," Low said. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht also came under pressure as players took long-dollar positions amid declines in other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Offshore banks sold dollars against the baht, pushing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 39.362 baht at the close, up from THB39.220 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar could move to test its year-high of THB39.470 against the baht Tuesday, one trader said. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar came under pressure amid weakness in the yen. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$30.812, up from NT$30.794 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar opened higher, but lost momentum in the afternoon following a weaker yen. The Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  often tracks the yen because Taiwan competes directly with the Japan export market. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won was the sole gainer among the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , moving higher following the release of stronger-than-expected trade data for June. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,113.80 won, down from Friday's close of KRW1,115.0. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires, 65 421-4859; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw73039c5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
