\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dollar Stronger Early On Suspected MAS Action \b0\par\par\uc2 180 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 2000\par 04:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar is stronger early Friday after suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversed a sudden overnight drop, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0220 GMT (10:20 p.m. EDT Thursday), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7335, down from S$1.7344 late Thursday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Singapore dollar dropped suddenly during London trading hours Thursday, driven down by stop-loss orders amid thin trading rather than on negative news. \par \par \uc2  After the U.S. dollar soared to S$1.7380, "Singapore banks all jumped in to sell aggressively," said a trader with a U.S. bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore, which doesn't comment on its actions, generally is said to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by placing orders through local banks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expect the U.S. dollar to continue trading within its recent range of about S$1.7300 to S$1.7360. \par \par \uc2  -By Christopher Wellisz, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4802; christopher.wellisz@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw6u0386v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
