\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Down Late; But S$ Bucks Trend \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 2000\par 11:24\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- While most other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  drifted lower late Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar defied the downward pressure, thanks to suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Domestic political and economic anxieties had stung the Thai baht, the Philippine peso and the Indonesian rupiah, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The MAS - which declined to comment Friday on its rumored actions - was said to have sold U.S. dollars when the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  climbed to a high of S$1.7380 in overnight trading. This pushed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back down to its recent trading level around S$1.7340, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  defended the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again around the level of S$1.7330 to S$1.7340 early Friday. Stop-loss orders were triggered when the U.S. dollar sank below S$1.7300, sending the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reeling to below the S$1.7380 support level, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "We do have a market that was caught long," said Alison Seng, an analyst at market consultants Standard & Poor's MMS International. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't herald a shift in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy, which is to allow the Singapore dollar to remain broadly stable against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Instead, the MAS probably wanted to put the market on notice that it wouldn't tolerate a significantly weaker Singapore dollar, several economists said. \par \par \uc2  Bank of America said it expects the U.S. dollar's next support level of around S$1.7250 to S$1.7260 to hold, ahead of a move back to S$1.7300. \par \par \uc2  Strong corporate U.S. dollar demand - needed to fulfill the recent string of overseas acquisitions - and expectations the Indonesian rupiah will weaken further will continue to weigh on the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Worries over the narrowing differentials between U.S. and domestic interest rates hammered the Philippine peso, dealers said. A lower-than-expected domestic oil price hike, however, helped the peso recoup some of its intraday losses, they added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 43.230 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from Thursday's close of PHP43.170. The dollar rose to an intraday high of PHP43.320 earlier in the day, its highest level since hitting PHP43.400 May 25. \par \par \uc2  The 55-centavo per liter oil price increase, lower than the expected PHP1.30 per liter, helped the peso recover some of its losses. Earlier Friday, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Rafael Buenaventura said an increase of PHP1.30 would have been inflationary. \par \par \uc2  In Thailand, political uncertainty, amid heightened fears of an early election, weighed on the baht. \par \par \uc2  Around 1000 GMT, the dollar was at 39.205 baht, up from THB39.165 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Mounting concerns over Indonesian President Abdurrahman Wahid's hold on power and whether the country could meet its economic reform deadlines by the middle of next week, which would pave the way for the disbursement of funds by the International Monetary Fund, weighed on the rupiah, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar-selling by Jakarta retail investors, however, capped the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ascent around the IDR8,745 to IDR8,755 level, dealers said. A lack of offshore buying interest, with the rupiah having moved within narrow ranges recently, also stymied the dollar's rise, they added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended Asian trading at IDR8,753, up slightly from IDR8,735 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar-demand by corporates that need to meet their debt repayment schedule and the political uncertainty will likely push the dollar higher to IDR8,800 next week, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won barely moved against the U.S. dollar as short-covering of dollars by local banks offset month-end dollar selling by local exporters, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,115 won, nearly flat from Thursday's close of KRW1,114.90. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar finished higher, bolstered by inward flows that could mean foreign investors are preparing funds to buy stocks at the start of the third quarter. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar finished lower at NT$30.794, compared with Thursday's close of NT$30.808. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw6u036gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
