\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dollar Up Late After Suspected MAS  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 514 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 2000\par 10:44\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar is higher late Friday after two \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0826 GMT (4:26 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7271, down from S$1.7344 late Thursday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Traders said, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York trading hours after the U.S. dollar shot up to S$1.7380, pushing it back down to its recent trading level of about S$1.7340, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Then, late Friday morning, the MAS was back in the market, selling U.S. dollars for Singapore dollars at S$1.7330, traders said. \par \par \uc2  However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expectedly declined to comment on the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "As a policy we don't usually comment," an MAS spokeswoman said. \par \par \uc2  The suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came as a surprise, since the MAS has stayed aloof from the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for weeks, allowing the Singapore dollar to drift lower against its U.S. counterpart. \par \par \uc2  Economists said, however, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't signal a shift in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy, which is to allow the Singapore dollar to remain broadly stable against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Neutrality with regards to the trade-weighted Singapore dollar policy remains in place," said David Simmonds, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  Like many regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Singapore dollar is lower against the U.S. dollar. But it is stronger against the euro and some other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , leaving it broadly unchanged in recent months. \par \par \uc2  Rather than signal a shift in policy, the MAS wanted to put the market on notice that it wouldn't tolerate a significantly weaker Singapore dollar, several economists said. \par \par \uc2  With interest rates in Singapore remaining low and those in the U.S. expected to continue rising, investors have been selling the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar and earning money on the interest rate spread. \par \par \uc2  The accumulation of such long U.S. dollar positions has threatened to push the U.S. dollar to levels that the MAS could no longer tolerate, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  In particular, the MAS doesn't want to see the U.S. dollar rise above S$1.7400, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's last peak in March 1999, said Steve Brice, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered Bank. \par \par \uc2  If the U.S. dollar does break through S$1.7400, it could then rise to the previous peak of S$1.7900, Brice said. "They are just aware that this is a key technical level and if they do go through, it would be that much harder to pull back," Brice said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the U.S. dollar is likely to drift lower in the short term as profitable long positions are liquidated for fear of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But several traders said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to find support again around S$1.7220 and start rising again from there. \par \par \uc2  -By Christopher Wellisz, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4802; christopher.wellisz@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw6u036e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
