\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dlr Up Early Amid Fears Of MAS  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 281 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 2000\par 05:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar regained some ground against its U.S. counterpart early Friday on fears the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore might step in to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At the opening, the U.S. dollar had climbed to as high as S$1.7363 on short-covering in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  However, fears that the MAS, the island-state's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  around S$1.7360, following rumors that they offered U.S. dollars around S$1.7360 to S$1.7365 Thursday, halted the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ascent Friday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There's talk the (de facto) \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is watching at levels above S$1.7360," a dealer at an Asian bank said, adding that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely be capped around there. \par \par \uc2  Around 0305 GMT (11:05 p.m. EDT Thursday), the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7340, down from S$1.7355 late Thursday in Asia, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese yen's resilience overnight also, though to a lesser extent, exerted some downward pressure on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , lending support to the Singapore dollar, dealers said. The U.S. dollar had broken below the key Y105 level overnight, and was trading at Y104.71 at 0300 GMT. \par \par \uc2  On the downside, corporate U.S. dollar demand around S$1.7320 to S$1.7330, apparently linked to recently announced merger and acquisition activities, is expected to lend support to the U.S. dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw6n032qd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
