\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Steady Early; Narrow Trading Band Seen \b0\par\par\uc2 492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2000\par 03:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are mostly steady early Wednesday, and are expected to remain wedged within narrow trading bands unless the U.S. dollar breaks key technical resistance levels, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Caution also prevails ahead of the Federal Reserve's Open Market Committee meeting June 27-28, amid uncertainty whether the Fed will pause its rate tightening cycle, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar-Asians will be well bid ahead of FOMC," a chief dealer at a European bank said. "Most market players are pricing in expectations that the Fed will leave rates unchanged, but there are fears the Fed may hike rates slightly, by 25 basis points - that could boost the dollar." \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. unit was little changed at S$1.7321, from S$1.7328 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could test the S$1.7350 resistance again later in the day, after it failed to breach the level Monday as offshore participants locked in profits. \par \par \uc2  Fears that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the S$1.7350 level could also cap the U.S. dollar's ascent, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Some U.S. dollar demand linked to Singapore Power's acquisition of Australian power transmission company GPU PowerNet for A$2.1 billion may have already been fulfilled at lower levels, some dealers say. Local banks had been actively bidding at lower levels last week - this could be related to the acquisition, they added. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains bullish following its recent rally against the Singapore dollar. The U.S. dollar is expected to find strong support at S$1.7280, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In South Korea, the dollar was barely changed at 1,119.7 won at 0150 GMT (9:50 p.m. EDT Tuesday), from Tuesday's close at KRW1,119.10, as the benchmark stock index on the Seoul bourse rose 1.2%. The dollar is expected to trade between KRW1,117 and KRW1,120, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Against the Thai bhat, the dollar was at 39.085 baht, little changed from THB39.080 late Tuesday. Keen corporate demand for dollars is expected around THB39.000-THB39.010, while offshore selling will likely cap the dollar at THB39.200, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In Manila, the dollar was at 42.650 pesos, down slightly from Tuesday's close at PHP42.675. The dollar is expected to trade in a PHP42.60-PHP42.70 range in the morning session, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah has been buoyed by reports Indonesian President Abdurrahman Wahid will reshuffle his cabinet after a key meeting in August of the country's highest legislative body. Wahid cited lack of cooperation among ministers as the reason for the proposed reshuffle. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 8,590 rupiah, down from IDR8,623 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw6l03239\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
