\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Steady Early Despite Decline In Rupiah \b0\par\par\uc2 193 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2000\par 04:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar is steady early Tuesday despite a drop in the Indonesian rupiah, which often roils Singapore's market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0230 GMT (10:30 p.m. EDT Monday), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7330, down slightly from S$1.7336 late Monday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they were surprised that the Singapore dollar has remained steady, given the U.S. dollar's strength against the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar briefly rose above 8,700 rupiah on market talk - later denied - that the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor would resign. Late Monday, the U.S. dollar was traded in Asia at IDR8,623. \par \par \uc2  One \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading desk chief said that concerns about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  are supporting the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Another dealer cited month-end selling of U.S. dollars by Singapore exporters. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expect the U.S. dollar to continue trading in a relatively narrow range of between S$1.7300 and S$1.7350. \par \par \uc2  -By Christopher Wellisz; 65/ 421-4802; christopher.wellisz@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5u02nwn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
