\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Down Early On US Rate Hike, But Off Low \b0\par\par\uc2 299 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2000\par 04:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar slipped early Wednesday on concerns of further tightening in the U.S. after the Federal Reserve raised rates by half a percentage point overnight, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Profit-taking in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, capped it at S$1.7335, its intraday high so far, in earlier trading. \par \par \uc2  At 0235 GMT (10:35 p.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar has retreated to S$1.7325, but still above S$1.7280 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Some funds are selling back (U.S.) dollars after the FOMC (Federal Open Market Committee) rate hike," a dealer at a U.S. bank said, adding he was unsure how long this will continue. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said speculators are hoping to buy on dips, with buying interest likely to emerge when the U.S. dollar weakens to S$1.7310. But Alison Seng, an analyst at MMS International, said it would be a "better entry level" at S$1.7260 to S$1.7270. \par \par \uc2  Market watchers said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely trade between S$1.7280 and S$1.7350 in the short term, with the Singapore unit expected to track the flagging Thai baht and Indonesian rupiah. They added that fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the quasi \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cap the dollar's rise at $1.7350. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment is still quite bullish for the (U.S.) dollar overall against the Asian units," the dealer added. \par \par \uc2  Seng said the Federal Open Market Committee, which is the Fed's policy-making arm, will likely raise interest rates by another 25 basis points during its next meeting June 27-28. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail; 65 421 4807 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5h02f0g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
