\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Down Late; Rupiah Casts Pall Over Region \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 2000\par 12:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The foundering Indonesian rupiah Monday cast a pall over regional Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  already under pressure from what analysts say is an impending hike in U.S. interest rates later this week. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah has taken a pummeling in recent weeks amid growing investor skepticism about the Indonesian government's ability to push ahead with reforms and keep the economic recovery on track. \par \par \uc2  President Abdurrahman Wahid said Monday he had no plan of action to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling further, but added he would be discussing the situation with his economic ministers in the coming days if the weakness persists. \par \par \uc2  An outbreak of rioting in the streets of Jakarta's Chinatown over the weekend provided the latest impetus for market players to dump the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday, the dollar was quoted at 8,541 rupiah, up from IDR8,485 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been pushed to seven-month low of IDR8,768 to a dollar at one point last Friday following a confusing barrage of comments by Indonesian policy makers. Wahid helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebound at the day's end by saying the government was prepared to act if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained weak. \par \par \uc2  That sparked speculation that he was prepared to impose some type of capital controls, but on Monday he said his comments were misinterpreted. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar couldn't escape the gravitational pull of the falling rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7296, up from S$1.7243 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Although the Singapore dollar mimicked the rupiah, its swings were far more moderate, and its losses were capped by expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  above the level of S$1.7300. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said there was a chance that the Singapore dollar and rupiah could get a boost if investors move to liquidate their long U.S. dollar positions ahead of Tuesday's Federal Open Market Committee meeting in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Market players have been snapping up U.S. dollars on expectations that the FOMC will raise short-term rates by a half percentage point, and that it likely will continue raising rates later in the year. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso also suffered under the weight of the expected narrowing of interest rate differentials with the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Profit-taking by banks long on dollars capped the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains, however, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 41.560 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from Friday's close at PHP41.500. \par \par \uc2  Concerns over the conflict in southern Mindanao between Muslim rebels and government troops, as well as questions on the stability of the banking industry, have been brushed aside for the time being, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won slipped to its lowest closing level against the U.S. dollar in nearly eight weeks on dollar buying by overseas players in the non-deliverable forward market. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,114.70 won, up from KRW1,114.50 on Friday. It was the highest close for the U.S. dollar since March 21 when it reached KRW1,116.40. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar ended weaker on concerns over relations with China and the ailing stock markets in Taiwan. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$30.767, up from NT$30.735 Friday. Trading was light as many market participants were sidelined, awaiting President-elect Chen Shui-bian's inauguration speech Saturday, hoping for clues on future relations with China, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Rupiah weakness weighed on the Thai baht, which breached the a key support level of 39.200 to a dollar. Lack of liquidity in the rupiah forced some dealers to sell the baht in a proxy move. \par \par \uc2  In late trading the dollar was quoted at 39.250 baht, up from THB38.845 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr. 65 421-4859;alan.yonan@dowjons.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5f02egn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
